Found by WI-borib re-taking WI-vosop's harness-vs-survey diff edge by
edge on the recorded aardvark-dns crate (docs/audits/0026).

1. ORDER. `use crate::config::parse_configs; parse_configs(..)` was
   looked up by its bare name, and the resolver's exact-name entry is
   whichever same-named function registered LAST: under walk order
   config/mod.rs, under a survey's sorted FileIndex order a
   #[cfg(test)] helper of the same name. One analyzer, two call graphs.
   A non-method call now resolves the absolute path its use bindings
   give (`h()` after `use a::b::h`, `m::h()` after `use a::m`, or a path
   from crate / self / super) to the function in the module FILE that
   path names (_rust_use_path_function over WI-rilof's _InTreeCrates and
   #1947's _rust_function_in_module). Positive evidence only: the module
   must resolve to a file and that file declare exactly one free function
   of the name; anything else -- an external path, `Thing::new` -- falls
   to the existing strategies unchanged.
2. SELF-BINDING. `self.0.push(err)` inside `fn push` and
   `self.items.push(x)` (items: Vec<u8>) bound the enclosing push, or
   another type's: the typed lookup `Vec::push` missed and the short-name
   fallback took any `push`. Two parts: tuple structs now register their
   fields by position ("0", "1", ...; the old docstring said
   `self.0.method()` "doesn't apply"); and when the receiver's type is
   KNOWN EXTERNAL -- a prelude type, a path rooted outside the tree, a
   name a `use` brought in from outside it -- the short name binds
   nothing. A type parameter (`backend: B`) is not that evidence: the
   first draft treated it as external and lost firecracker's
   Vsock<B>.backend.as_raw_fd -> TestBackend and sent ripgrep's
   `self.sink.matched(` to the enclosing Core.matched; caught in the A/B
   and pinned by a control test.

